<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

/**
 * Source-first Batch 694 item definitions. Answer text is stored separately
 * from distractors so the authoring runner can distribute answer positions.
 * Academic review remains pending; the cited PRC/DepEd materials establish
 * scope and curricular grounding, not independent approval of each item.
 */
return [
    'values_rewrites' => [
        [5818, 'values-personhood-and-dignity', 'easy', 'During a class discussion, a learner with a speech device is interrupted before finishing. The class has time for only two more comments. What response best protects participation and dignity?', 'Pause the discussion, restore the learner’s turn, and allow the device the time it needs.', ['Invite a volunteer to summarize what the learner probably intended so the schedule stays on track.', 'Move to the next speaker, then offer the learner a private chance to explain after class.', 'Ask the group to vote on whether the unfinished comment should be heard now or later.'], 'Equal participation requires access to the same discussion, with reasonable time for the learner to communicate. A summary or private follow-up can substitute others’ judgment for the learner’s own contribution.'],
        [5819, 'values-hierarchy-and-conflicts', 'medium', 'A class officer receives a message that a student plans to share another student’s private health information in a group chat. The student says the post is “just a joke.” What should the officer weigh first?', 'The affected student’s privacy and safety, the likely harm, and the school’s proper support route.', ['Whether the message is funny to most classmates before deciding if the disclosure matters.', 'Whether the officer can delete the post personally without involving anyone else.', 'Whether the student who sent the message has previously been disciplined.'], 'A sound decision starts with affected people, rights, foreseeable harm, and authorized procedures. Popularity, a joke label, or past character judgments do not settle the present privacy concern.'],
        [5820, 'values-filipino-cultural-analysis', 'medium', 'A student describes “pakikisama” as agreeing with friends even when their plan excludes a new classmate. Which teaching prompt best supports careful analysis of the value?', 'Ask what relationship and community harmony require when exclusion harms someone’s dignity.', ['Tell the student that pakikisama always means following the majority’s preference.', 'Ask the class to avoid discussing Filipino values because people interpret them differently.', 'Have the excluded classmate decide whether the group’s conduct represents Filipino culture.'], 'Values should be interpreted in context and examined for their effects on relationships and dignity. No single behavior represents a whole culture, and agreement is not the only way to sustain community.'],
        [5821, 'values-psychological-principles', 'easy', 'A learner says, “I failed once, so I am a dishonest person forever,” after accidentally submitting an uncited paragraph. What response best supports moral learning?', 'Separate the mistake from the learner’s identity, then help identify a repair and a future citation habit.', ['Agree that one dishonest act proves a fixed character trait that will not change.', 'Dismiss the concern because the paragraph was only one part of the assignment.', 'Require a public apology before discussing what happened or what can be repaired.'], 'A developmental approach treats behavior as evidence for reflection and change, not as a permanent label. The learner can acknowledge the effect, repair the work, and practice a reliable citation process.'],
        [5822, 'values-hierarchy-and-conflicts', 'hard', 'A teacher has one accessible device for an activity. Two learners need it for different reasons; one has a formal accommodation and the other has an urgent task requirement. What is the fairest next step?', 'Clarify each learner’s need and timing, consult the support plan, and arrange an equitable sequence or alternative.', ['Give it to the learner with the formal accommodation without checking the task timing or alternatives.', 'Use a first-come rule because applying one rule to everyone is always the fairest choice.', 'Let the learners negotiate privately so the teacher does not influence the decision.'], 'Fairness attends to relevant differences, the agreed support plan, urgency, and practical alternatives. Identical treatment can leave a real access need unmet; private bargaining can shift responsibility onto learners.'],
        [5823, 'values-personhood-and-dignity', 'medium', 'A student volunteers to care for younger siblings after school, but the duty is affecting sleep and assignment completion. Which conference question best respects the student’s agency while examining responsibility?', '“What parts of this duty can you influence, and what support would help you protect rest and learning?”', ['“Why are you not refusing the family duty if schoolwork is important to you?”', '“Would it be easier if you stopped mentioning the family responsibility at school?”', '“Can you promise to finish every assignment even when the schedule changes?”'], 'The question recognizes family responsibility and the learner’s needs without assuming control over family circumstances. It opens a concrete discussion of support, tradeoffs, and realistic choices.'],
        [5824, 'values-psychological-principles', 'medium', 'A class interprets one learner’s silence as agreement. Later the learner explains that they were translating the discussion mentally before responding. What should the teacher do next?', 'Offer adequate response time and more than one participation mode, then check understanding without assigning a motive.', ['Record the silence as agreement because no objection was made during the discussion.', 'Call on the learner without notice each time so the class can observe their response.', 'Ask classmates to explain the learner’s communication style for future lessons.'], 'Observable silence does not establish agreement or a psychological trait. Flexible participation and a direct, respectful check avoid making assumptions about the learner.'],
        [5825, 'values-filipino-cultural-analysis', 'medium', 'A learner says that “utang na loob” requires accepting a relative’s request to copy an assignment. Which response best distinguishes gratitude from academic responsibility?', 'Invite the learner to acknowledge the help received while explaining why submitting copied work would be unfair.', ['Say that family loyalty overrides school rules whenever a relative asks for help.', 'Tell the learner that gratitude is irrelevant and should not influence family relationships.', 'Ask the relative to decide whether copying is acceptable in this particular case.'], 'Gratitude can be expressed without abandoning fairness or academic integrity. The decision should consider the relationship and the effects on learning and other students, rather than treating a value as an automatic command.'],
        [5826, 'values-hierarchy-and-conflicts', 'hard', 'A student reports a harmful rumor about a classmate but asks the teacher not to tell anyone. The rumor is spreading and may expose the classmate to harassment. Which response best handles the competing duties?', 'Listen privately, explain the limits of confidentiality, assess immediate risk, and share only necessary facts through the authorized safeguarding route.', ['Promise absolute secrecy so the student will remain willing to speak with the teacher.', 'Tell the whole class the report so students can correct the rumor together.', 'Wait for the targeted classmate to complain before taking any protective step.'], 'Trust matters, but it does not justify promising secrecy where safety may be at risk. A limited, need-to-know response protects the student while avoiding unnecessary disclosure.'],
        [5827, 'values-intrapersonal-relations', 'easy', 'A learner is angry after receiving peer feedback and wants to send a reply immediately. Which self-management step is most useful before responding?', 'Name the emotion, identify the specific feedback, and decide what information needs clarification.', ['Send the first reply that expresses the full intensity of the anger.', 'Avoid the peer for the rest of the term so another disagreement cannot occur.', 'Ask another learner to send a response on the student’s behalf without permission.'], 'Recognizing emotion and separating it from the feedback helps the learner choose a deliberate response. Avoidance or delegation does not resolve the concern or build self-understanding.'],
        [5828, 'values-interpersonal-relations', 'medium', 'Two group members disagree about who should present their project. One says the other “always wants attention,” while the other points to the assignment rubric. What facilitation move best restores a respectful discussion?', 'Restate the shared task, ask each person for relevant reasons, and apply the presentation criteria transparently.', ['Decide based on which student has spoken less during earlier group meetings.', 'Ask each student to list the other person’s past mistakes before choosing a presenter.', 'Let the loudest student present because the group needs to reach a quick decision.'], 'A transparent criterion addresses the decision and gives both students a fair chance to contribute. Personal labels and volume are poor substitutes for relevant reasons.'],
        [5829, 'values-family-moral-reasoning', 'medium', 'A learner says a family rule prevents them from joining a school event, but does not want the teacher to contact home yet. What should the teacher do first?', 'Ask what the learner is comfortable sharing, identify any immediate safety concern, and explain available options.', ['Contact the family immediately and describe the learner’s comments in detail.', 'Tell the learner that school participation should take priority over family rules.', 'Assume the rule is unfair and encourage the learner to ignore it.'], 'The teacher should listen and understand the student’s context before taking action, while explaining that safety concerns may require support. Assumptions can undermine trust and cultural respect.'],
        [5830, 'values-self-understanding', 'easy', 'A student chooses a career path mainly because friends have chosen it. Which reflection prompt best supports self-understanding without dismissing peer relationships?', '“Which parts of this path fit your interests and strengths, and which parts come mainly from your friends’ plans?”', ['“Why are you copying your friends instead of making an independent choice?”', '“Would your friends still support you if you chose a different course?”', '“Which career has the highest status among the people you know?”'], 'The prompt helps distinguish personal interests from social influence without treating relationships as inherently bad. Career reflection should use the learner’s own evidence and priorities.'],
        [5831, 'values-intrapersonal-relations', 'medium', 'After making a mistake in a community activity, a learner says, “I should never volunteer again.” Which response best turns reflection into learning?', 'Ask what happened, what effect it had, and what manageable step could improve the next attempt.', ['Agree that avoiding future participation is the safest response to a mistake.', 'Focus only on the learner’s good intention and skip the impact on others.', 'Ask the group to decide whether the learner deserves another opportunity.'], 'Reflection considers both intention and impact, then identifies a specific next action. A global judgment about worth or avoidance prevents useful learning.'],
        [5832, 'values-family-moral-reasoning', 'hard', 'A learner is pressured by relatives to keep a family disagreement private, while the conflict is disrupting the learner’s wellbeing at school. Which response best respects family bonds and the learner’s welfare?', 'Explore the learner’s wishes, risks, and trusted supports, then agree on a safe and limited next step.', ['Insist that the learner disclose every detail to the school because privacy is less important.', 'Treat family loyalty as a reason to stop discussing the matter at school.', 'Contact relatives without telling the learner so adults can settle the disagreement.'], 'Family relationships deserve respect, but privacy does not require leaving a learner unsupported. A safe, collaborative next step preserves agency while addressing wellbeing.'],
        [5833, 'values-self-understanding', 'medium', 'A learner wants to apologize after interrupting a classmate but worries that a public apology would embarrass the classmate again. What should the learner consider?', 'Ask what form of acknowledgment would be respectful to the classmate and take responsibility without seeking praise.', ['Make a public apology so everyone knows the learner has corrected the mistake.', 'Say nothing because apologizing can draw attention to the incident.', 'Ask the classmate to confirm the apology in front of the group.'], 'Repair should address the affected person’s needs rather than the apologizer’s image. A private, direct acknowledgment may be more respectful when public attention would repeat the harm.'],
        [5834, 'values-interpersonal-relations', 'easy', 'In a peer discussion, a student strongly rejects a classmate’s argument but wants to keep the group working together. Which opening best supports constructive disagreement?', '“I understand your concern about cost; can we compare how each option affects access?”', ['“You are missing the point, so let us use my option and finish.”', '“Everyone knows your suggestion will not work in this class.”', '“I will agree for now, but I will tell the teacher later that it was wrong.”'], 'The opening acknowledges the other person’s concern, states an issue that can be examined, and keeps the disagreement about the proposal rather than the person.'],
        [5835, 'values-intrapersonal-relations', 'medium', 'A student feels responsible for resolving every conflict among friends. One friend asks the student to take sides in a disagreement. What boundary is most constructive?', 'Listen without spreading private details, explain that the student will not decide who is right, and suggest a direct conversation or trusted mediator.', ['Choose the friend who shared the first account because loyalty should follow the first disclosure.', 'Repeat both accounts to the rest of the group so everyone can judge the conflict.', 'End both friendships until the friends agree on what happened.'], 'A boundary can preserve care while refusing an unfair role. Sharing private details or deciding based on timing increases harm instead of helping the friends address the disagreement.'],
        [5836, 'values-filipino-value-system', 'medium', 'A community meeting opens with a customary greeting, but some participants do not share the same religious practice. Which facilitator choice best combines cultural respect with inclusion?', 'Offer the customary greeting as context, make participation optional, and provide a respectful nonreligious way to begin.', ['Require every participant to repeat the greeting so the meeting feels unified.', 'Remove all cultural references because any shared practice may exclude someone.', 'Ask participants who differ to arrive after the opening so the program can continue.'], 'Inclusion can preserve cultural context without requiring uniform belief or participation. An optional and respectful opening avoids both compulsion and unnecessary erasure.'],
        [5837, 'values-rights-and-citizenship', 'hard', 'Students want to publish a complaint about a school facility. Their draft names a staff member as responsible, but they have not checked the maintenance records. What should the group do before publishing?', 'Verify the relevant facts, remove unsupported personal claims, and state the concern through an appropriate public channel.', ['Publish the draft immediately because public criticism is protected even when details are unchecked.', 'Cancel the complaint because naming any school problem is disrespectful.', 'Ask classmates to vote on whether the named staff member seems responsible.'], 'Responsible participation protects expression while taking care not to spread unsupported allegations. Verification and a proportionate channel improve the credibility of the complaint.'],
        [5838, 'values-peace-gender-environment', 'medium', 'During a planning meeting, a student dismisses a classmate’s suggestion as “not for girls.” The classmate asks to continue the discussion without being singled out. What should the facilitator do?', 'Set a clear equal-participation norm, redirect the group to the proposal’s merits, and check privately what support is wanted.', ['Ask the classmate to defend why girls should be included before the group continues.', 'Ignore the remark because the classmate asked not to be singled out.', 'End the meeting and report the speaker without clarifying what happened.'], 'A facilitator can address discriminatory conduct as a group norm without making the affected student responsible for educating others. A private check respects the student’s stated preference.'],
        [5839, 'values-filipino-value-system', 'easy', 'A group interprets “bayanihan” as asking the same few volunteers to do every task because they have helped before. Which question best tests whether the practice reflects shared responsibility?', '“How can the work be distributed so people contribute according to their capacity and no one is taken for granted?”', ['“Which volunteer is most dependable, so the group can assign them the difficult tasks again?”', '“Can the group keep the familiar arrangement if the project is completed on time?”', '“Should people who cannot volunteer be excluded from the project decisions?”'], 'Shared assistance depends on reciprocal, fair participation rather than repeatedly relying on the same people. Capacity and voice matter alongside the project outcome.'],
        [5840, 'values-rights-and-citizenship', 'medium', 'A student council receives a proposal to ban phones during lunch after several students filmed peers without permission. What process best balances privacy, participation, and practical enforcement?', 'Gather affected students’ views, review school rules, and consider a targeted privacy rule with clear reporting and enforcement.', ['Adopt a total ban immediately because one harmful use proves all phone use is unsafe.', 'Reject any restriction because students’ personal devices are outside school responsibility.', 'Let each class choose a different rule without a shared privacy standard.'], 'A proportionate process addresses the documented privacy harm while considering student participation and consistent administration. Both blanket prohibition and no response ignore important parts of the issue.'],
        [5841, 'values-peace-gender-environment', 'medium', 'A class is choosing between a low-cost event that creates substantial disposable waste and a more expensive reusable setup. The budget cannot cover every request. Which deliberation is most responsible?', 'Compare whole-life costs, expected waste, accessibility, and feasible reuse options before choosing a scaled plan.', ['Choose the lowest immediate price and treat later waste as someone else’s concern.', 'Choose the reusable setup regardless of affordability or who can access the event.', 'Cancel the event because any environmental impact would undermine its purpose.'], 'Responsible stewardship considers environmental effects and actual resources. A scaled, accessible plan can reduce waste without pretending budget limits or tradeoffs do not exist.'],
        [5842, 'values-rights-and-citizenship', 'medium', 'A class wants to include a student’s personal story in a campaign poster about discrimination. The story could make the message powerful, but the student has not agreed to publication. What should the group do?', 'Use the story only after obtaining informed permission and agreeing on what details may be shared.', ['Publish the story without a name because classmates will recognize the event anyway.', 'Ask the teacher to approve publication because a good cause outweighs individual choice.', 'Replace the story with an invented account that implies the student’s experience.'], 'A public-interest goal does not remove the storyteller’s control over personal information. Permission should cover the use and detail, and a general campaign can proceed without appropriating the story.'],
        [5843, 'values-peace-gender-environment', 'hard', 'Two student groups dispute a shared garden space. One group prioritizes food production; the other uses it for quiet, accessible outdoor activities. What mediation step should come first?', 'Clarify each group’s needs, access requirements, and constraints before developing options for shared or scheduled use.', ['Give the larger group the full space because majority support is easier to implement.', 'Divide the space equally without checking paths, growing needs, or accessibility.', 'Ask one group to leave for the sake of harmony and avoid further disagreement.'], 'A fair resolution begins by understanding the distinct needs and constraints. Equal square footage or quick agreement can still preserve unequal access or fail both purposes.'],
        [5844, 'values-self-understanding', 'medium', 'A learner has changed career interests after a work-exposure activity. A relative says changing plans shows a lack of commitment. Which reflection best supports a considered decision?', 'Compare the new experience with the learner’s earlier assumptions, strengths, and current responsibilities before revising the plan.', ['Keep the first plan because changing it would disappoint the relative.', 'Choose the new path immediately because personal interest should outweigh every constraint.', 'Wait for other people to agree before considering what the experience revealed.'], 'Career reflection can revise a plan when new evidence changes the learner’s understanding. It should consider both personal fit and realistic responsibilities, rather than freezing or impulsively replacing a decision.'],
        [5845, 'values-career-development', 'easy', 'A learner is choosing a practicum placement. One offers a prestigious title but little supervised work; another offers regular feedback on the skills the learner wants to develop. Which factor should guide the choice?', 'The learning opportunities and supervision that match the learner’s goals, alongside practical conditions.', ['The placement’s public image because prestige is the clearest measure of career value.', 'The option preferred by friends because shared placements make adjustment easier.', 'The shorter commute alone because the placement’s learning content can be learned later.'], 'Career development weighs skill-building and support with practical realities. Prestige, peer preference, or convenience alone do not reveal which placement advances the learner’s goals.'],
        [5846, 'values-community-service-in-teaching', 'medium', 'A school plans a weekend clean-up and asks a teacher to require every student to attend. Some students have caregiving duties or transport barriers. How should the activity be organized?', 'Offer meaningful roles and alternatives, make expectations clear, and coordinate participation so access does not depend on personal resources.', ['Require attendance and treat any absence as lack of concern for the community.', 'Cancel the activity because voluntary participation makes community service ineffective.', 'Let students with fewer obligations complete the work while others receive credit.'], 'Community service should contribute to shared goals without creating an unfair access test. Flexible roles and alternatives preserve purpose while recognizing unequal circumstances.'],
        [5847, 'values-digital-work-ethics', 'hard', 'A teacher receives a spreadsheet with student grades in a shared folder. A colleague asks for a copy on a personal messaging account to “work faster.” What is the best response?', 'Use the school-approved access method, verify the colleague’s need, and share only the records required for the task.', ['Send the full spreadsheet because both teachers work at the same school.', 'Refuse all collaboration because student grades are always confidential from colleagues.', 'Take a screenshot of the relevant rows and send it through the personal account.'], 'Professional information should be accessed for a legitimate role through appropriate safeguards and limited to what is needed. A personal channel may expose more data than the task requires.'],
        [5848, 'values-workplace-professional-conduct', 'medium', 'A new teacher notices that a colleague has been assigned extra unpaid tasks because the colleague is described as “always willing to help.” What is a fair professional response?', 'Check the task allocation and workload process, then raise the concern through a respectful, appropriate channel.', ['Ask the colleague to refuse every extra task without first understanding the assignment process.', 'Accept the arrangement because reliable staff should carry more of the shared work.', 'Tell students about the staffing concern so they can pressure the school to act.'], 'Work ethics includes fair and transparent workload practices. The concern should be checked and raised through suitable channels, without shifting all responsibility to the colleague or exposing staff matters to students.'],
        [5849, 'values-career-development', 'medium', 'A graduate receives two job offers. One has higher pay but unclear duties; the other has lower starting pay, documented mentoring, and a defined review date. What comparison is most useful?', 'Compare total conditions, role clarity, development support, and the graduate’s needs over the stated period.', ['Choose the higher salary because future learning can be pursued after accepting.', 'Choose the mentoring role because development support outweighs any financial need.', 'Ask family members to choose the offer with the better reputation.'], 'A career decision should compare several relevant dimensions and the person’s constraints. Neither income nor mentorship alone settles the decision for every graduate.'],
        [5850, 'values-community-service-in-teaching', 'medium', 'A community group asks a class to help design a reading activity. Adults disagree about which local stories may be used. What is the most respectful next step?', 'Ask community members about permissions, intended use, and attribution, then co-design an activity within those limits.', ['Choose the story that best matches the lesson and add a general acknowledgment later.', 'Avoid local stories entirely because permissions could delay the activity.', 'Select a story privately with the most vocal adult so planning can continue.'], 'Co-design makes consent, attribution, and local knowledge part of the activity. A general acknowledgment cannot replace permission, while avoidance may unnecessarily exclude community resources.'],
        [5851, 'values-digital-work-ethics', 'medium', 'A school staff member discovers that an online form collects student birth dates although the activity only needs grade level. What should the staff member do?', 'Ask the responsible team to review the purpose and remove unnecessary fields before collecting more responses.', ['Keep the form unchanged because more information could be useful for future programs.', 'Delete all responses immediately without checking records or authorized procedures.', 'Tell students to enter approximate birth dates so the form can be submitted.'], 'Data minimization starts with the stated purpose. The team should limit collection and handle existing records under the proper process, rather than gather extras or destroy data without authorization.'],
        [5852, 'values-workplace-professional-conduct', 'hard', 'A teacher’s public post criticizes a school decision and includes a screenshot of an internal message. The concern is legitimate, but the screenshot identifies a student. What is the most responsible next action?', 'Remove or restrict the identifying material, preserve the concern, and raise it through an appropriate channel without unnecessary student disclosure.', ['Leave the post unchanged because the decision deserves public scrutiny.', 'Delete the entire concern and make no further report because a privacy mistake occurred.', 'Replace the student’s name with initials while leaving other identifying details visible.'], 'The public concern can be raised without exposing a student. Limiting identifying details and using a suitable channel addresses both accountability and privacy.'],
    ],

    'values_new' => [
        ['values-personhood-and-dignity','easy','A learner asks for a quiet place to complete a reflection after a distressing event. The school room is being used for a scheduled group. What should the teacher explore first?','What immediate support and privacy the learner needs, and which available space can safely provide it.',['Whether the learner can wait until the group has finished without explaining what is wrong.','Whether the learner’s classmates agree that a quiet place is necessary.','Whether the teacher can move the group without checking their activity needs.'],'The first step is to understand the learner’s immediate need and available safe options. A vote or an unexamined delay makes access depend on others’ judgment.','medium'],
        ['values-personhood-and-dignity','medium','A class is making a profile of “successful students.” Most examples describe learners who speak confidently in public. How can the class improve the profile?','Ask which abilities the examples miss and include different ways learners contribute and show understanding.',['Keep the examples because public speaking is the most visible form of success.','Remove individual examples and describe success only through grades.','Ask the quietest learners to explain why they do not speak more often.'],'A broader profile challenges a narrow measure without replacing it with another single measure. Learners should not be made responsible for justifying an identity or communication style.','medium'],
        ['values-personhood-and-dignity','hard','During a peer review, one learner says a classmate’s religious practice is “backward.” The classmate wants the work reviewed but does not want a debate about belief. What should the teacher do?','Stop the demeaning remark, return feedback to the work criteria, and check privately what support the classmate wants.',['Ask the classmate to explain the practice so peers can decide whether the remark was accurate.','Ignore the comment and continue because the classmate asked not to debate belief.','Cancel all peer review so no one has to discuss different beliefs.'],'Respect protects the learner from demeaning treatment while honoring the request to focus on the work. Silence can normalize the insult; a broad cancellation avoids rather than repairs the classroom norm.','hard'],
        ['values-personhood-and-dignity','medium','A teacher is preparing a case discussion about disability. Which choice best treats learners as persons rather than as examples of a category?','Use a varied case and ask learners to analyze barriers, choices, and supports without assigning one voice to a whole group.',['Ask a learner with a disability to speak for people with the same diagnosis.','Use a single success story to show that effort can overcome any barrier.','Avoid disability in the lesson so no student feels personally addressed.'],'A person-centered discussion examines context and agency while avoiding stereotypes or tokenization. Neither one story nor exclusion supports a full analysis.','medium'],
        ['values-personhood-and-dignity','easy','A student declines to join a class prayer but is willing to help with the event setup. What arrangement best respects both conscience and participation?','Allow the student to opt out of the prayer and take part in a nonreligious event role.',['Require attendance at the prayer because the activity is part of a class event.','Exclude the student from the entire event to avoid distinguishing participation.','Ask classmates to decide whether the student’s reason is sincere.'],'Respect for conscience can coexist with meaningful participation. A whole-event exclusion or peer judgment is not necessary to accommodate a religious difference.','easy'],
        ['values-personhood-and-dignity','medium','A class activity asks learners to disclose a personal hardship. One learner prefers to analyze a fictional case. How should the teacher respond?','Accept the fictional case if it meets the learning objective and does not require personal disclosure.',['Require a personal example so the reflection feels authentic to the teacher.','Excuse the learner from the activity because the class uses personal reflection.','Ask the learner to disclose only a less serious hardship instead.'],'Learning can be assessed without compelling private disclosure. A fictional case can support the same reasoning while preserving the learner’s boundaries.','medium'],
        ['values-hierarchy-and-conflicts','medium','A student sees a friend take a small item from a shared classroom supply box, then hear the friend say they will return it tomorrow. What is the most responsible first response?','Ask the friend about the item and the shared-use expectation, then help return it or clarify the procedure.',['Report the friend publicly as a thief before checking why the item was taken.','Ignore it because the item is inexpensive and will probably be returned.','Take another item from the box so the supply remains evenly distributed.'],'The response checks facts and the shared rule while supporting repair. Public accusation without inquiry and silent tolerance fail to address the responsibility.','medium'],
        ['values-hierarchy-and-conflicts','hard','A student council must allocate limited project funds between an accessibility improvement and a popular event. Both proposals have documented benefits. Which process is fairest?','Use announced criteria that include access, expected benefit, feasibility, and who would otherwise be excluded.',['Allocate funds to the event that received the most votes, regardless of access needs.','Split the budget equally even if neither proposal can then be completed.','Choose the proposal supported by the council chair to avoid disagreement.'],'Transparent, relevant criteria let the group compare benefits and exclusion under scarcity. A vote or equal split may be useful evidence, but neither automatically addresses fairness or feasibility.','hard'],
        ['values-hierarchy-and-conflicts','medium','A student hears a classmate claim that a rule was applied unfairly. The student was not present when the decision was made. How should the student respond?','Listen to the concern, distinguish what is known from what is reported, and help identify the proper review process.',['Repeat the claim as confirmed because the classmate sounds certain.','Reject the concern because the student did not witness the decision.','Post a poll asking classmates whether the rule was unfair.'],'A careful response takes the concern seriously without turning a report into a proven fact. A proper review can examine evidence and process more fairly than rumor or dismissal.','medium'],
        ['values-hierarchy-and-conflicts','medium','A classmate asks a student to share a teacher’s private feedback message to prove that a grading dispute is real. What should the student do?','Ask what evidence is needed and share only the relevant information through an authorized review route.',['Forward the full message to the class group because the dispute concerns fairness.','Refuse to discuss the grade at all because any feedback is private.','Edit the message to remove the teacher’s name and post it publicly.'],'The student can support fair review while limiting disclosure to the appropriate people and purpose. Full forwarding or public editing may expose more information than necessary.','medium'],
        ['values-hierarchy-and-conflicts','hard','A student volunteers to translate for a peer during a sensitive meeting. The peer agrees, but the student is also part of the dispute being discussed. What is the best next step?','Check whether an impartial interpreter or trusted adult is available and let the peer choose with full information.',['Proceed because the peer agreed and the volunteer understands the language.','Cancel the meeting because translation creates a conflict that cannot be managed.','Ask the volunteer to interpret only the parts that seem relevant.'],'Consent matters, but so does impartiality and accurate communication. An independent option supports informed choice; selective interpretation risks altering the peer’s meaning.','hard'],
        ['values-filipino-cultural-analysis','medium','A class compares “hiya” in two short narratives. In one, concern about embarrassment prevents a learner from asking a needed question; in the other, it prompts care about another person’s dignity. What conclusion is best supported?','The value’s effects depend on context, relationships, and how it shapes the available choices.',['The value always prevents people from acting honestly.','The two stories cannot be compared because values are entirely personal.','The second story proves that avoiding embarrassment is always respectful.'],'The same concept can influence behavior differently across situations. Contextual analysis avoids both a fixed stereotype and the claim that comparison is impossible.','medium'],
        ['values-filipino-cultural-analysis','hard','A community organizer says “pakikipagkapwa” means newcomers should follow long-standing group practices without question. Which response best examines the claim?','Ask how shared personhood and mutual regard can include newcomers’ perspectives while preserving valued practices.',['Agree because a shared identity depends on newcomers adopting existing customs.','Reject the concept because any community practice can exclude someone.','Ask newcomers alone to decide which traditions the whole group should retain.'],'The analysis asks whether a practice reflects mutual regard and genuine participation. It avoids making conformity the test of belonging or transferring all cultural decisions to newcomers.','hard'],
        ['values-filipino-cultural-analysis','easy','A learner says “respect for elders” means an adult’s instruction should never be questioned, even when it conflicts with a safety rule. Which prompt encourages sound analysis?','“How can respect be shown while asking for clarification about the safety concern?”',['“Which elder in the community has the greatest authority to settle the question?”','“Why do younger people need to challenge traditions at all?”','“Can respect be measured by how quickly someone obeys?”'],'Respectful communication and safety can be considered together. Authority or speed of obedience alone does not show whether the instruction is safe or how concern should be raised.','easy'],
        ['values-filipino-cultural-analysis','medium','A class studies “bayanihan” through a story in which neighbors help after a flood. Which question moves beyond praising cooperation?','“How were roles, resources, and decisions shared, and whose needs were not yet met?”',['“Which neighbor showed the strongest Filipino character?”','“Would the story be less meaningful if help came from an organization?”','“How quickly did the neighbors finish the cleanup?”'],'The question examines participation, distribution, and unmet need. A moral label or speed measure can miss whether the response was inclusive and effective.','medium'],
        ['values-filipino-cultural-analysis','medium','Students compare family expectations about caring for relatives. Some learners live in extended households and others do not. How should the discussion proceed?','Use varied, voluntary examples and examine duties and constraints without treating one household form as the norm.',['Ask students from extended households to explain what all families should do.','Use only one family model so the class can reach a consistent conclusion.','Avoid the topic because family responsibilities differ too much to discuss.'],'A varied and voluntary discussion can compare responsibility while respecting different circumstances. No learner should speak for a whole group or have their family model treated as universal.','medium'],
        ['values-intrapersonal-relations','easy','A learner notices that they agree with a proposal mainly because a close friend supports it. Which private reflection is most useful?','List the reasons that matter to the learner and check whether those reasons would still support the proposal without the friendship.',['Avoid the proposal so the friendship cannot influence the decision.','Ask other friends which choice would protect the relationship best.','Treat agreement as proof that the friendship is healthy.'],'The reflection identifies social influence without assuming it invalidates the choice. The learner can consider evidence and priorities while still valuing the relationship.','easy'],
        ['values-intrapersonal-relations','medium','A student is asked to join a protest during school hours but is unsure about the issue. What supports responsible self-directed judgment?','Review reliable information, clarify personal values, and understand the school’s participation rules before deciding.',['Join immediately because refusing may disappoint the organizer.','Stay away from all civic activity until every participant agrees on the facts.','Post a strong opinion first so others know the student is engaged.'],'An informed decision connects evidence, values, and relevant responsibilities. Immediate conformity or public performance does not establish that the learner understands the issue.','medium'],
        ['values-intrapersonal-relations','medium','A learner feels guilty for setting a boundary with a friend who repeatedly asks for completed homework. What reflection helps distinguish care from enabling?', 'How can the learner support the friend’s learning without surrendering responsibility for their own work?',['Would the friend remain close if the learner stopped helping?','Can the learner share only the answers to the easiest questions?','Should the teacher be asked to make the boundary decision for both friends?'],'Care can include constructive support and a clear academic-integrity boundary. Fear of rejection, partial answer sharing, or outsourcing the decision does not address the underlying learning need.','medium'],
        ['values-intrapersonal-relations','hard','A student is upset about an unfair comment and wants to confront the speaker in front of the class. Which action best balances self-respect and a fair chance to resolve the issue?','Describe the comment and its effect calmly, choose a suitable setting, and state what change is being requested.',['Wait until the speaker makes another comment so the pattern is easier to prove.','Ask the class to judge the comment before speaking to the person.','Send an angry message immediately so the speaker understands the harm.'],'A specific, proportionate conversation communicates the harm and a possible repair without turning the issue into a public trial or allowing it to continue without response.','hard'],
        ['values-intrapersonal-relations','medium','A learner’s plan to study nightly keeps failing because of an unpredictable commute. Which self-reflection is most likely to lead to a usable change?','Identify the actual time and energy available, then choose a smaller routine with a cue and a backup plan.',['Repeat the same plan with more discipline because the goal is important.','Give up the goal because a routine should work every day to be useful.','Ask a classmate to remind the learner each night without changing the schedule.'],'Reflection should use evidence about context and constraints. A smaller routine with a trigger and fallback is more actionable than self-blame or abandoning the goal.','medium'],
        ['values-intrapersonal-relations','easy','A learner receives praise for a group result but knows that another member did most of the work. What response supports honest self-understanding?','Acknowledge the team’s contribution and describe the learner’s own role accurately.',['Accept the praise because the whole group received the same score.','Redirect all praise to the other member and deny any work the learner did.','Stay silent so the teacher can decide how credit should be assigned.'],'Accurate acknowledgment avoids taking undeserved credit while still recognizing the learner’s actual contribution. Honesty does not require either exaggeration or self-erasure.','easy'],
        ['values-family-moral-reasoning','medium','A student is asked to keep a younger sibling safe during an unexpected work shift for a parent. The student also has an important exam the next morning. What should the teacher explore with the student?','What immediate care is needed, what trusted support is available, and how the school can help protect learning and safety.',['Whether the student is willing to give up the exam because family duty comes first.','Whether the parent can be reported for asking the student to help.','Whether the student can study during the caregiving period without any support.'],'The facts and safety needs come first, followed by realistic support and a plan for learning. A forced ranking of family and school responsibilities or an assumption about blame is premature.','hard'],
        ['values-family-moral-reasoning','medium','A learner says a family member has chosen a course for them and that refusing may create conflict. What question best supports agency without prescribing a family decision?','“What matters to you about each option, and what kind of conversation or support would make it safer to discuss?”',['“Which course will make your family most proud?”','“Why not choose what you want and explain it after enrollment?”','“Can a teacher contact your family and tell them which course is suitable?”'],'The prompt helps the learner identify values and possible support while respecting the family relationship. It does not assume confrontation or transfer the decision to the teacher.','medium'],
        ['values-family-moral-reasoning','hard','A student learns that a cousin is being pressured to share private photographs. The cousin asks the student not to involve adults. What should the student consider first?','Whether there is immediate danger or coercion, what the cousin wants, and which trusted safeguarding support can protect them.',['Promise secrecy and wait for the cousin to decide if the pressure becomes serious.','Confront the person pressuring the cousin without considering safety or evidence.','Tell the wider family group so everyone can help decide what happened.'],'The student should not promise secrecy where coercion or safety may be at stake. A limited trusted-support route can protect the cousin while avoiding broad disclosure or a risky confrontation.','hard'],
        ['values-family-moral-reasoning','easy','A learner’s family uses a language that differs from the language of school communication. What is the most respectful way to include the family in a conference?','Ask what language or interpretation support would make the discussion clear and invite the family’s questions.',['Use the school language more slowly and assume that this will be enough.','Ask the learner to translate sensitive details for the adults.','Send a written summary after the conference instead of checking understanding.'],'Meaningful participation requires communication the family can use. Slowing speech, relying on the learner for sensitive interpretation, or sending a summary alone may not support informed discussion.','easy'],
        ['values-family-moral-reasoning','medium','A parent objects to a classroom discussion because it conflicts with family beliefs, while students are studying how to compare ethical arguments. What should the teacher do?','Explain the learning purpose and boundaries, listen to the concern, and discuss a reasonable way for the student to meet the objective.',['Remove all discussion of values so no family belief is challenged.','Tell the parent that the classroom’s values always override family values.','Ask the student to defend the family’s position to classmates.'],'The response respects family concerns and the educational objective while seeking a workable, non-tokenizing arrangement. It avoids suppressing the subject or forcing the student to represent a family view.','medium'],
        ['values-self-understanding','medium','A learner’s journal shows that they feel energized when solving practical problems but drained by long, unstructured meetings. How can this evidence inform career exploration?','Use it as one clue about preferred tasks and environments, then compare it with skills, values, and realistic opportunities.',['Treat it as proof that the learner should avoid every career involving meetings.','Ignore it because interests change and cannot guide a career decision.','Choose a career based on the task that currently feels easiest.'],'Self-observation is useful evidence but not a final diagnosis or career rule. It should be compared with abilities, commitments, and the actual requirements of different roles.','medium'],
        ['values-self-understanding','easy','A student is deciding whether to accept a leadership role. They value service but worry that they will neglect their studies. Which reflection best clarifies the decision?','Identify the responsibilities, supports, time limits, and learning goals attached to the role.',['Accept because serving others is always more important than personal plans.','Decline because a responsible learner should never risk being busy.','Ask the most popular classmate whether the role is worth taking.'],'The reflection connects a value to real duties and constraints. Neither automatic acceptance nor avoidance tests whether the role can be undertaken responsibly.','easy'],
        ['values-self-understanding','medium','A learner has received feedback that their group work is reliable but that they rarely ask for help. Which goal is most useful for the next project?','Set a specific point to request clarification or support before a task becomes difficult.',['Try to complete the whole project alone so the group can rely on the learner.','Ask for help on every task so no uncertainty remains.','Wait for another member to notice when the learner needs support.'],'A specific help-seeking goal builds on reliability while addressing an identified pattern. Both complete independence and unbounded help-seeking miss the learner’s need for timely, appropriate support.','medium'],
        ['values-self-understanding','hard','A graduating student wants a career that serves the public but is uncertain about the working conditions they can sustain. Which next step supports a responsible choice?', 'Gather first-hand information about role expectations, accessibility, workload, and development paths, then compare those facts with personal priorities.',['Choose the role with the broadest public impact before learning its daily requirements.','Reject public-service work because uncertain working conditions may become difficult.','Rely on a job title to infer whether the role will fit the student’s needs.'],'A responsible career choice uses evidence about actual work as well as values. A title or broad mission cannot show whether the specific role is sustainable or suitable.','hard'],
        ['values-filipino-value-system','medium','A class studies “malasakit” through a volunteer project where organizers make decisions without asking residents. Which question best tests whether concern became responsible action?','“How were residents’ priorities heard, and did the project respond to the need they identified?”',['“How many organizers worked late to prepare the project?”','“Did the activity receive positive comments on social media?”','“Was the project completed without changing the original plan?”'],'Concern is shown through attention to people’s stated needs and the effects of action. Effort, publicity, or adherence to a plan does not alone establish that the project helped.','medium'],
        ['values-filipino-value-system','hard','A student describes “diskarte” as finding a way to pass a task by using a classmate’s answer key. Which teaching response best analyzes the value without endorsing the shortcut?', 'Distinguish resourcefulness from unfair advantage by examining the process, consent, and effect on other learners.',['Praise the outcome because creativity matters more than how a solution is reached.','Reject the concept because it can be used to excuse dishonest conduct.','Ask whether the student was likely to be caught before judging the choice.'],'Resourcefulness can be evaluated alongside fairness and integrity. The analysis avoids both blanket praise and blanket rejection, and it does not treat detection as the moral standard.','hard'],
        ['values-filipino-value-system','medium','In a community project, elders recommend one location while youth identify a transport barrier there. How can the group honor tradition and include the youth’s evidence?','Discuss the recommendation together, examine the access evidence, and consider an adjustment that preserves the project’s purpose.',['Keep the location unchanged because elders have already reached a decision.','Move the project immediately because any access concern settles the question.','Ask youth to collect more evidence only after the project begins.'],'Respectful deliberation gives both experience and current access evidence a place in the decision. The purpose may be preserved through a reasoned adjustment rather than automatic deference or reaction.','medium'],
        ['values-filipino-value-system','easy','A learner says “pakikiramdam” means noticing that a teammate seems uncomfortable, but is unsure what to do. What is the most respectful next step?','Check in privately, offer a choice about continuing, and avoid claiming to know the teammate’s feelings.',['Speak for the teammate during the meeting so they do not have to explain.','Ignore the discomfort until the teammate makes a direct complaint.','Tell the group that the teammate is uncomfortable and needs a break.'],'Sensitivity can prompt a respectful check without turning inference into certainty or disclosing someone else’s feelings. A direct, private offer preserves agency.','easy'],
        ['values-filipino-value-system','medium','A school heritage event includes a tradition that some learners experience differently. What makes the class discussion academically responsible?','Describe the tradition’s context, invite multiple evidence-based perspectives, and avoid making one learner the representative.',['Ask the learner most connected to the tradition to decide what it means for everyone.','Present one interpretation as authentic because it comes from a long-standing account.','Avoid asking questions because respectful study requires agreement.'],'Responsible cultural analysis treats traditions as contextual and open to evidence while respecting people’s varied relationships to them. One account or one learner cannot speak for everyone.','medium'],
        ['values-rights-and-citizenship','medium','A student council collects signatures for a petition. A learner wants to sign but worries that the list will be posted publicly with names. What should the council provide?','Explain how names will be used, offer a private or anonymous way to express support where feasible, and state any limits.',['Post the full list because public names make a petition more credible.','Remove the petition because participation should never involve identifying information.','Tell learners that signing is voluntary without describing how the list will be used.'],'Participation is more informed when learners understand the use of their information and available options. Voluntariness alone does not answer privacy questions.','medium'],
        ['values-rights-and-citizenship','hard','A group wants to challenge a local decision but has only one account of what happened. Which civic action is most defensible before making a public accusation?','Request the decision record, verify the timeline, and distinguish confirmed facts from questions that remain open.',['Publish the account immediately because delay may weaken public attention.','Avoid any public action until every person involved agrees about the decision.','Use anonymous claims from social media to fill gaps in the account.'],'Civic participation can proceed while maintaining factual care. Records and clear uncertainty help the group criticize a decision without presenting unverified allegations as established fact.','hard'],
        ['values-rights-and-citizenship','medium','A class is drafting a rule for use of a shared laboratory. One proposal restricts access to prevent damage, but it may exclude learners who need additional setup time. What should the class examine?','The safety purpose, affected users, available supervision, and a workable way to preserve equal access.',['Adopt the restriction because shared property should always be protected first.','Reject any restriction because equal access means identical use conditions.','Ask learners who need setup time to use the laboratory outside class.'],'A fair rule protects safety while examining how it affects access and what alternatives can meet both needs. Equal treatment does not always mean identical arrangements.','medium'],
        ['values-rights-and-citizenship','easy','A student discovers an incorrect figure in a class petition after several classmates have signed it. What should the student do?','Tell the organizers, correct the information, and give signers a chance to reconsider using the accurate version.',['Leave the figure because the petition’s main concern remains important.','Delete the petition without informing the classmates who signed it.','Change the figure quietly so the petition does not lose support.'],'Correction protects the integrity of collective participation. Signers should know what they are supporting; importance of a cause does not justify leaving or quietly altering an error.','easy'],
        ['values-peace-gender-environment','medium','A disagreement over a team role is becoming personal. One student proposes that each side list what they need from the role before discussing who should do it. What should the facilitator do?','Use the needs list to identify shared goals and options, then agree on a decision process.',['Choose the role assignment that ends the discussion fastest.','Ask each student to defend their character before discussing the task.','Separate the students for the whole project without checking whether that solves the problem.'],'A needs-based conversation redirects the dispute to the work and opens options. Speed or character judgments are not reliable substitutes for a fair process.','medium'],
        ['values-peace-gender-environment','hard','A class plans a debate on a divisive issue. Some learners worry that the format rewards quick speakers and public confrontation. How can the teacher preserve disagreement while making participation fairer?','Share evidence beforehand, allow written and spoken contributions, and set norms for challenging claims without attacking people.',['Cancel the debate and replace it with a lecture that gives one correct position.','Keep the format unchanged because equal time limits are fair to every speaker.','Let each learner choose a side publicly before the evidence is presented.'],'Multiple participation modes and clear discussion norms make reasoned disagreement more accessible without suppressing differing views. Equal time alone does not remove format barriers.','hard'],
        ['values-peace-gender-environment','medium','A student wants to correct a harmful online post but worries that replying publicly will spread it further. Which response should the student consider?', 'Preserve evidence privately, use the platform’s reporting tools, and check with the affected person about support before amplifying the post.',['Quote the full post and explain why it is harmful so more users can see the context.','Reply with an insult so the original poster understands the effect.','Say nothing because online conduct cannot affect classroom relationships.'],'A response can address harm while avoiding unnecessary amplification and respecting the affected person’s preferences. Public exposure or silence alone may not solve the problem.','medium'],
        ['values-peace-gender-environment','easy','A student group finds that a cleanup route is too steep for one volunteer using a mobility aid. What is the best planning response?','Choose an accessible route or divide tasks so the volunteer can contribute meaningfully without being singled out.',['Ask the volunteer to supervise from the starting point instead.','Keep the route and arrange for another volunteer to carry the equipment.','Remove the volunteer from the activity so the group can stay on schedule.'],'The group should design participation around access rather than assigning a lesser role or treating the person as cargo. A different route or task arrangement can preserve meaningful contribution.','easy'],
        ['values-peace-gender-environment','hard','A school wants to reduce electricity use, but some rooms become too hot for concentration when ventilation is limited. Which plan is most responsible?', 'Measure when and where energy is used, protect safe learning conditions, and test targeted changes with follow-up data.',['Set one lower thermostat setting for every room and treat discomfort as a temporary adjustment.','Cancel energy conservation because student comfort must always take priority.','Ask each classroom to choose its own practice without shared safety guidance.'],'Stewardship considers energy and health together. Measurements and targeted trials support a proportionate plan; blanket action or no shared guidance ignores context.','hard'],
        ['values-peace-gender-environment','medium','A student notices that a group’s “joke” about a classmate’s accent is making the classmate withdraw. What response best supports dignity and a peaceful classroom?', 'Name the impact without repeating the joke, set a respectful-language boundary, and check privately what repair the classmate wants.',['Repeat the joke more slowly so the group can discuss why it is funny.','Tell the classmate to ignore the comment to prevent further conflict.','Punish the whole group before learning who participated or what happened.'],'A clear boundary stops the harm while avoiding unnecessary repetition and collective assumptions. Private follow-up can identify a repair that respects the affected student.','medium'],
        ['values-career-development','medium','A learner is choosing between two training programs. One advertises rapid employment; the other publishes course outcomes and offers employer-supervised practice. What information should the learner compare?', 'Verify recognition, actual competencies, costs, placement terms, and evidence behind employment claims.',['Choose the program with the strongest employment slogan because it matches the goal.','Choose the longer program because more hours guarantee better skills.','Ask graduates only whether they enjoyed the course.'],'Career planning requires evidence about outcomes and practical conditions, not slogans or length alone. Graduate experience can help but should be part of a broader comparison.','medium'],
        ['values-career-development','easy','A student receives feedback that they communicate well in small groups but have difficulty presenting to a large audience. What career-development action uses this feedback constructively?', 'Choose a specific presentation skill to practice and seek gradual, supported opportunities to use it.',['Avoid roles involving communication until the difficulty disappears.','Ignore the feedback because communication skills cannot be improved.','Apply for only the most public-facing roles to overcome the weakness quickly.'],'Targeted practice builds from strengths and addresses a specific development need. Avoidance and abrupt exposure do not provide a measured learning plan.','easy'],
        ['values-career-development','medium','A school career fair offers a limited number of employer interviews. Several students have not had access to information about how to apply. What should organizers do?', 'Publish the criteria and preparation support early, then allocate interviews through the announced process.',['Give interviews to students who already know the employers so time is not lost.','Let the organizer choose students based on general impressions of readiness.','Open applications only during the fair so all learners begin at the same time.'],'Transparent criteria and accessible preparation reduce the advantage of prior connections. A fair start time does not address information gaps or arbitrary selection.','medium'],
        ['values-career-development','hard','A graduate is asked to accept a role with an unclear job description and a verbal promise of later promotion. Which question best protects informed career choice?', 'What are the written duties, compensation, support, evaluation criteria, and review date for the proposed role?',['How soon can the promotion be announced to other applicants?','Can the graduate trust the employer’s confidence and accept before details are set?','Would declining make the graduate appear insufficiently committed?'],'Specific terms let the graduate assess obligations and development prospects. Reputation pressure or a future promise cannot replace clear information about the current role.','hard'],
        ['values-career-development','medium','A learner’s interests point toward two different fields, both of which require further training. How can the learner make a grounded next decision?', 'Compare training requirements and try a low-risk activity from each field before choosing a longer path.',['Choose the field with more public recognition before learning its daily work.','Delay all exploration until the learner can be certain about a lifelong career.','Ask family members to choose which training the learner should pursue.'],'Small, relevant experiments provide evidence without demanding certainty. Recognition and others’ preferences may matter, but they do not reveal fit or requirements by themselves.','medium'],
        ['values-community-service-in-teaching','medium','A teacher plans a reading session with a community center. The center asks that children not be photographed. What should the teacher include in the plan?', 'A no-photo procedure, an alternative record of participation, and clear communication to all helpers.',['Take group photographs without names because faces alone are not personal information.','Ask each child at the event whether a photo may be posted publicly.','Cancel the activity because documentation is part of school reporting.'],'The host’s condition and children’s privacy should shape documentation from the start. A last-minute request or an assumption about photographs may not provide appropriate consent or safeguards.','medium'],
        ['values-community-service-in-teaching','hard','A community partner asks students to distribute donated supplies, but the listed recipients include people who did not request assistance. What is the most responsible step?', 'Coordinate with the partner to verify need and consent, then distribute supplies through a respectful process.',['Deliver every item as listed because the partner already has the names.','Ask students to decide which recipients appear most in need.','Publish the recipient list so community members can correct it.'],'Verification and respectful consent reduce misallocation and avoid exposing personal circumstances. Student impressions and public lists are not reliable or privacy-conscious substitutes.','hard'],
        ['values-community-service-in-teaching','medium','A class can support a reading program either by collecting books or by helping children use books already available. How should the class choose?', 'Ask the partner about current needs and choose the contribution that supports the program without duplicating supplies.',['Collect books because a visible donation is easier to celebrate.','Choose the activity students enjoy most without consulting the partner.','Do both tasks even if the partner cannot store or supervise the materials.'],'Service should respond to the community’s actual needs and capacity. Visibility or enthusiasm is useful only when it supports the purpose and can be managed.','medium'],
        ['values-community-service-in-teaching','easy','A student misses a service activity because their transport was canceled. What should the teacher do when assessing participation?', 'Use the stated learning criteria and offer an equivalent way to demonstrate the relevant contribution or reflection.',['Record no participation because every student was offered the same activity.','Excuse the student from all future community work.','Give full credit without checking whether the learning objective was met.'],'Fair assessment considers access and the objective. An equivalent option can measure learning without ignoring either the barrier or the expected work.','easy'],
        ['values-digital-work-ethics','hard','A teacher wants to use an online tool that stores student work. The service requests broad permission to use submissions for product improvement. What should the teacher do before uploading work?', 'Review the school’s approved tools and data rules, clarify the service terms, and avoid uploading identifiable work without authorization.',['Upload sample work first because the service may remove names automatically.','Accept the terms because a free tool can improve student learning.','Ask students to consent individually without reviewing school responsibilities.'],'The teacher must assess purpose, data handling, institutional rules, and safeguards before sharing student work. Removing names or seeking individual consent alone may not address all risks.','hard'],
        ['values-digital-work-ethics','medium','A staff group uses a shared password for a classroom account. A member leaves the school. What should the account owner do?', 'Change the shared credential, review access, and move to individual authorized accounts where the system supports them.',['Keep the password because the account contains only teaching resources.','Send the password to the new staff member and ask them to keep it private.','Delete the account immediately without checking for required school records.'],'Access should be limited to current authorized users and the account owner should preserve needed records through the proper process. A shared password gives no reliable way to revoke one user.','medium'],
        ['values-digital-work-ethics','medium','A teacher receives a message from an unknown account claiming to be a parent and requesting a student’s schedule. What is the safest professional response?', 'Verify the sender through an established school channel and share no schedule until the request is authorized.',['Send the schedule but omit the student’s full name.','Ask the student to confirm whether the account belongs to their parent.','Reply with the schedule and request identification afterward.'],'Verification must come before disclosure. Partial identifiers or student confirmation through an unverified channel may still expose a student’s movements.','medium'],
        ['values-workplace-professional-conduct','medium','A school team discovers that its published event date conflicts with a community partner’s confirmed schedule. The publicity materials are already printed. What should the team do?', 'Confirm the conflict, notify affected participants, and issue a corrected schedule through the same channels.',['Keep the published date because changing it may look disorganized.','Change the date online but leave printed materials uncorrected.','Ask the partner to adjust their confirmed schedule to match the school.'],'Professional responsibility includes accurate information and timely correction across channels. Appearance or convenience does not justify leaving participants with conflicting details.','medium'],
        ['values-workplace-professional-conduct','hard','A teacher is offered a gift by a supplier whose product is being considered for a school purchase. What is the most responsible response?', 'Decline or disclose the offer through the applicable school process and avoid participating in a decision affected by the conflict.',['Accept the gift if it is described as a courtesy and decide impartially later.','Ask a colleague to make the purchase decision while keeping the gift private.','Accept the gift and share it with the school so no one person benefits.'],'A connected gift can undermine impartiality even when it is shared. Disclosure and the relevant conflict process protect the public decision and the teacher.','hard'],
        ['values-workplace-professional-conduct','easy','A new staff member notices that a colleague’s meeting notes omit a decision that affects their assigned work. What should the staff member do?', 'Ask the note-taker to clarify and correct the record so responsibilities are clear to everyone affected.',['Assume the omission was intentional and raise a formal complaint immediately.','Accept the notes because the staff member was not the meeting chair.','Send a separate version to the whole school with the missing decision added.'],'A direct clarification can correct the record proportionately and keep affected colleagues informed. An accusation or competing record may create confusion before the facts are checked.','easy'],
        ['values-workplace-professional-conduct','medium','A teacher cannot meet a deadline because an emergency duty has displaced planned work. What is the most professional response?', 'Notify the responsible person early, state what is complete, and agree on a realistic revised deadline or handoff.',['Wait until the deadline passes so the teacher can report a complete result later.','Submit unfinished work without noting which parts still need review.','Ask a colleague to submit the work under the teacher’s name.'],'Early communication supports continuity and honest expectations. Silence, unmarked incompleteness, or misattributed work can transfer risk to others.','medium'],
        ['values-digital-work-ethics','medium','A teacher wants to use an AI writing tool to prepare feedback on student essays. What practice best protects fairness and professional judgment?', 'Check the school’s rules, avoid sharing identifying work without authorization, and review every suggestion before giving feedback.',['Paste full essays into any available tool because feedback remains educational.','Use the tool’s score as the final grade because it treats every essay consistently.','Avoid telling students that technology assisted any part of the feedback.'],'Professional judgment and data safeguards remain the teacher’s responsibility. Consistency does not prove validity, and unapproved disclosure can expose student work.','medium'],
        ['values-rights-and-citizenship','hard','A student council collects signatures for a proposal. After submission, the secretary notices that one sentence was changed from the version students reviewed. The revised text still supports the same goal. What is the fairest response?','Disclose the change, ask signers whether they still support the revised wording, and correct the record before presenting it as their petition.',['Keep the signatures because the proposal’s purpose has not changed.','Remove the sentence quietly so the petition does not lose support.','Ask only the council officers to approve the change on behalf of all signers.'],'Participation depends on knowing what one is endorsing. A material wording change requires a transparent chance to reaffirm support; officers cannot assume each signer agrees.','hard'],
        ['values-peace-gender-environment','medium','A school plans a reusable-container campaign, but the nearby vendor has no washing station and students have limited access to clean water. What should the organizers do before requiring the containers?','Consult affected students and vendors, assess safe cleaning options, and pilot a workable plan before setting a requirement.',['Require the containers immediately because waste reduction is the only relevant value.','Cancel the campaign because any access constraint makes reuse impractical.','Ask students to bring containers but leave cleaning responsibility undefined.'],'Environmental goals should be pursued alongside health, access, and practical conditions. Consultation and a bounded pilot can reveal whether the proposed practice is safe and feasible.','medium'],
    ],

    'psych_development_new' => [
        ['psych-development-theories','easy','Researchers measure the same children’s vocabulary every six months from ages three to five. Which design best describes this approach?','A longitudinal design follows the same participants across time.',['A cross-sectional design compares different age groups at one point.','A case study examines one participant in unusual detail.','A natural experiment assigns children to different age groups.'],'Repeated measurement of the same participants over time is longitudinal. The design can show individual change, although attrition and repeated testing must be considered.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-research-ethics','medium','A study compares the reading scores of two age groups tested in different calendar years. The groups had different access to remote learning. What is the main interpretive concern?','Cohort and historical differences may contribute to the score gap as well as age.',['The score difference proves that reading ability changes only with age.','The two groups cannot be compared because developmental research never uses age groups.','The reading test measures physical rather than cognitive development.'],'Age and historical context are confounded in a cross-sectional comparison. The scores alone do not identify which factor produced the difference.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-theories','medium','An infant gradually becomes more accurate at reaching for objects over several months, with no clear point at which the skill suddenly appears. Which view best fits this pattern?','A continuous account describes gradual accumulation of skill across time.',['A stage-only account requires a sudden change at one fixed age.','A discontinuous account treats every small improvement as a new stage.','A cohort account explains the change through a historical generation.'],'The described gradual improvement is consistent with continuous development. It does not rule out stages in other abilities or imply that every learner follows the same pace.','easy'],
        ['psych-development-nature-nurture','hard','Two children exposed to the same classroom experience respond differently; one has a history of hearing impairment and the other does not. What is the strongest developmental inference?','Development reflects interacting individual and environmental conditions, so the same experience need not have the same effect.',['The classroom experience caused the difference because it occurred before both responses.','The children’s outcomes are fixed by biology and cannot be influenced by instruction.','The difference shows that developmental research cannot study learning environments.'],'The contrast illustrates interaction between learner characteristics and experience. It does not isolate one cause or imply that outcomes are predetermined.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-nature-nurture','medium','A pregnant patient asks whether one medication exposure guarantees a developmental disorder. What is the most scientifically careful reply?','Exposure can affect risk, but outcome depends on factors such as dose, timing, and individual context; consult a qualified clinician.',['Any exposure guarantees the same outcome for every fetus.','No exposure can matter unless a visible difference appears at birth.','The exposure’s effect can be diagnosed from the medication name alone.'],'Developmental risk is not the same as certainty. Timing and dose matter, and individual medical advice is outside a general study explanation.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-theories','medium','A toddler checks a caregiver’s face before approaching an unfamiliar toy, then explores while returning for reassurance. Which interpretation is most consistent with attachment theory?','The caregiver is functioning as a secure base that supports exploration and checking back.',['The toddler has no attachment because independent exploration occurs.','The toddler is showing separation anxiety because any caregiver check is distress.','The toddler has mastered object permanence because the toy is unfamiliar.'],'Secure-base behavior combines exploration with access to a caregiver. It should be interpreted from a pattern of interaction, not a single action.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-theories','easy','An infant searches for a toy after watching it hidden under a cloth. Which cognitive ability does the behavior most directly suggest?','Object permanence: the object is represented as continuing to exist when unseen.',['Conservation: quantity remains stable despite a change in appearance.','Egocentrism: another person’s viewpoint is difficult to consider.','Reversibility: a mental operation can be undone.'],'Searching for the hidden object is evidence of representing it while out of sight. The other terms describe different cognitive tasks.','easy'],
        ['psych-development-theories','medium','A child says a taller glass has more juice even after watching the same juice poured from a shorter glass. Which concept is the child still developing?','Conservation of quantity across a change in container shape.',['Object permanence across a change in visibility.','Secure attachment across a change in caregiver distance.','Phonological awareness across a change in word order.'],'The amount is unchanged while the container shape changes. The response reflects difficulty coordinating appearance and quantity, not memory of an unseen object.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-theories','medium','A child recognizes that a classmate may believe a toy is in a drawer because the classmate did not see it moved. Which ability is being demonstrated?','Theory of mind: reasoning about another person’s belief and knowledge.',['Conservation: recognizing that quantity is unchanged after transformation.','Habituation: responding less to a repeated stimulus.','Secure-base behavior: using a caregiver during exploration.'],'The child distinguishes their own knowledge from the classmate’s belief. That is a perspective-taking task.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-theories','hard','A learner solves a puzzle only after an instructor models one step and offers prompts that are gradually withdrawn. Which concept best describes the temporary support?','Scaffolding within the learner’s zone of proximal development.',['A fixed stage that makes instruction unnecessary until a new age.','Classical conditioning through repeated pairing of a cue and response.','A norm-referenced score showing performance compared with peers.'],'Temporary support helps a learner perform a task not yet manageable independently, then fades as skill develops. It is not a score or an age-fixed stage.','hard'],
        ['psych-development-theories','medium','An adolescent explores several possible identities before making a commitment. In Erikson’s framework, which developmental task is most relevant?','Identity formation in adolescence.',['Trust formation in infancy.','Intimacy development in early adulthood.','Integrity review in later adulthood.'],'Exploration of roles and commitments is associated with the identity task. The framework describes a developmental challenge, not a diagnostic label.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-nature-nurture','medium','A school asks learners to describe the same event from different social roles. Several accounts differ without any learner being factually dishonest. What is the most useful interpretation?','Experience and perspective can shape which parts of an event people notice and recall.',['Only the first account is valid because later accounts are influenced by peers.','The differences prove memory is unreliable for every purpose.','Each account must describe a different event because perspectives cannot overlap.'],'Perspective can influence attention and interpretation while accounts may still share facts. The differences call for careful comparison, not automatic dismissal.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-theories','medium','A child’s vocabulary grows after many conversations, with new words building on existing language knowledge. Which developmental pattern does this illustrate?','Cumulative change is consistent with a continuous developmental process.',['A sharp stage transition is required whenever a new word is learned.','A cohort effect explains the child’s language growth.','A critical period proves later language learning is impossible.'],'The example describes gradual accumulation. It does not show that development has no qualitative shifts or that a fixed period makes later learning impossible.','easy'],
        ['psych-development-theories','medium','Two adolescents of the same age differ in emotional regulation and independence. Which conclusion is most defensible?','Age provides context, but development varies with individual and social experience.',['One adolescent must be outside the normal developmental range.','Both adolescents should have identical skills because age determines development.','The difference can only be explained by a biological disorder.'],'Age-linked patterns are broad guides, not individual guarantees. Context and variability matter, and difference alone does not establish abnormality.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-nature-nurture','hard','An older adult takes longer to learn a new phone interface but draws on extensive vocabulary and workplace knowledge to solve problems. Which distinction best explains the mixed pattern?','Some cognitive abilities change differently with age; accumulated knowledge can remain a strength while processing speed changes.',['All cognitive abilities decline together at the same rate.','Accumulated knowledge proves there is no age-related change.','A slower response shows the person has lost the ability to learn.'],'Development is multidimensional. A change in speed does not imply that knowledge or learning capacity is absent.','hard'],
        ['psych-development-theories','medium','An older adult chooses a smaller circle of close friends and spends more time with them. Which account is most consistent with socioemotional selectivity theory?','Perceived time horizons can shift social goals toward emotionally meaningful relationships.',['Older adults lose the ability to form new relationships.','Friendship count alone determines psychosocial health at every age.','The adult is showing an attachment disorder because the network is smaller.'],'The theory concerns changing social priorities, not a universal loss of social ability. Relationship quality and context matter alongside network size.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-nature-nurture','hard','A child who was once slow to warm up becomes more comfortable after a caregiver and teacher coordinate predictable introductions. What does this example support?', 'Temperament may shape behavior, while supportive environments can influence how it is expressed.',['Temperament is fixed, so the change cannot be developmental.','The environment fully caused the child’s temperament.','The child’s earlier behavior proves a permanent social limitation.'],'The example is consistent with interaction: a relatively stable disposition and environmental support both matter. It does not identify a single cause or a permanent limit.','hard'],
        ['psych-development-nature-nurture','medium','A child learns a local counting strategy from grandparents and later uses it to explain a school math problem. Which perspective best highlights the social context of development?','A sociocultural view emphasizes learning through cultural tools and interaction.',['A maturation-only view attributes the strategy solely to age.','A trait-only view explains the strategy as a stable personality feature.','A cohort-only view attributes it solely to the child’s birth year.'],'Social interaction and culturally available tools can shape how a learner thinks. Age, traits, and cohort may matter, but they do not explain the described learning process alone.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-theories','hard','A child says a rule is unfair because it benefits the child’s own team, then later recognizes that the same rule would disadvantage another team. Which conclusion is most careful?', 'The child is developing moral perspective-taking; a single response does not establish a fixed stage or character.',['The child has reached a permanent level of moral reasoning.','The child’s first answer proves that the child lacks empathy.','The child’s later answer shows that social context is irrelevant.'],'The change suggests emerging perspective-taking, but one exchange is not enough to label a person or establish a fixed developmental stage.','hard'],
        ['psych-development-research-ethics','hard','A researcher wants to observe preschoolers on a playground without telling families because the setting is public. What must be considered before beginning?', 'Applicable consent, child assent where appropriate, privacy, risk, and the ethics review requirements for the study.',['No review is needed because playground behavior is visible to other people.','Only the teacher’s permission matters because children attend the school.','The researcher can collect names and video if the results are for education.'],'Public visibility does not remove ethical duties. Research with children requires attention to consent, assent, privacy, risk, and institutional review.','hard'],
        ['psych-development-research-ethics','medium','A developmental study finds an association between screen time and sleep in a survey completed once by families. What conclusion is justified?', 'The variables are associated in this sample, but the survey does not by itself establish which caused the other.',['Screen time caused the sleep difference in every child.','Poor sleep caused families to report more screen time.','The association proves that screen use has no developmental relevance.'],'A one-time observational association cannot determine direction or rule out other explanations. It can motivate further study without settling causation.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-research-ethics','medium','A team translates a child-development questionnaire for use in a community speaking another language. What should the team do before comparing scores directly?', 'Check translation meaning, cultural relevance, and whether the measure functions comparably in the new context.',['Use direct translation only because the original measure was standardized.','Compare raw scores first and investigate differences if results look unusual.','Remove culturally specific items without checking whether the construct changes.'],'Translation is not enough to establish equivalent measurement. Meaning, relevance, and comparability should be examined before interpreting group differences.','hard'],
        ['psych-development-research-ethics','medium','A teacher observes that one student began reading later than classmates and asks whether this means the student has a disorder. What is the most appropriate response?', 'A single milestone comparison is not a diagnosis; gather broader evidence and refer concerns through qualified assessment procedures.',['Yes, later reading than classmates is sufficient evidence for diagnosis.','No, reading development is too variable to assess at any age.','The teacher should wait until the student fails several more grades.'],'Milestones can inform questions but do not diagnose an individual. Broader evidence and qualified procedures are needed, without either premature labeling or avoidable delay.','medium'],
        ['psych-development-research-ethics','hard','A researcher’s longitudinal study loses several participants who moved to another area. Why should the final report describe this attrition?', 'Who remains may differ from who left, which can affect whether observed change represents the original sample.',['Attrition matters only when the sample becomes too small to calculate an average.','Participants who leave should be treated as having the same outcomes as those who remain.','Longitudinal data cannot be interpreted after any participant withdraws.'],'Attrition can introduce bias if withdrawal relates to study variables. Reporting it helps readers evaluate the limits without making the entire design unusable.','hard'],
    ],
];
